#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133882 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133698 :question/id #uuid "69c1029c-777f-5a16-8ff5-9f8f0c9a48b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9071" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,0)$ and $(0,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\cos{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y}= \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\,\\textrm d A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a78a98750e3203760d27daaebd42852198538935e2c3777a0b8a8c85d1bb0901.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)= \\cos{y}$ and $Q(x,y)= x\\sin{y}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y) = 2\\sin{y}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf385144-2f48-5874-8c0d-dec31d8fc6e3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048655 :topic/math-academy-id 3356 :topic/title "Simple, Closed, and Oriented Curves"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050666 :topic/math-academy-id 3705 :topic/title "Sums of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Over Parametric Curves"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38de2abc-a7b1-5d3a-8957-d1ab9e5fb1ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241691} {:answer/id #uuid "40e4b86c-bcbb-5404-8559-d21f662ac62b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}({y}^{3}+{x}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241762} {:answer/id #uuid "657bd0c3-147f-5fc7-9596-1342db9f58cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242091} {:answer/id #uuid "c432a38f-4f2b-5dfa-8915-d2bb0d8669d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242903} {:answer/id #uuid "e27db473-126a-5337-8f68-decd55d0a82a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(y-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38de2abc-a7b1-5d3a-8957-d1ab9e5fb1ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241691} :answer-field/id #uuid "63363597-99c8-5060-88d1-1a0669413b41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243630}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00a6ca58-bb1e-5472-9379-ee67967bcfed" :question/math-academy-id "q-108588" :question/problem "Consider the square $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(0,1),(1,0)$ and $(1,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+{x}^{2}\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/aa/aa60dd35a920d166f7cc3238a366bde93bd2c33e1b39b0a38313ab46a8de2d8b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2(x-y)\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2(x-y).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134075 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17ebf303-f63a-56d2-84bc-bbaa1d0e9a51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241386} {:answer/id #uuid "795e8e88-c522-5db6-90b6-0eabc5c96150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242249} {:answer/id #uuid "8dbff0ef-e453-5de9-b0b2-d423d9e27b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242427} {:answer/id #uuid "cde97e8e-d758-5755-a2d7-f375bdd195e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242994} {:answer/id #uuid "d9edd3d8-8ce5-5d63-b21b-565e48377530" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9edd3d8-8ce5-5d63-b21b-565e48377530" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243121} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b1f949a-6640-5db4-b58d-c6d2f237a658" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243516}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b395a92-9fb2-5e47-8972-f8a867f5011a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109161" :question/problem "Consider the path $C,$ in the plane, as the boundary of the region enclosed by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y\\,dx+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/c4/c44b320dd7ba2824ae0024a3d77d0c6cd6e06fa5889e23879f3d6f464c3a80df.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y\\,dx+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y-(-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y+2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ee90334-9a31-5d11-96f1-a862c794ad22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241596} {:answer/id #uuid "9b9964f5-61c7-5dad-85cd-0db18b40db50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242540} {:answer/id #uuid "c4759efa-de8a-5792-950e-a484236ceef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242909} {:answer/id #uuid "c4be2895-6c99-5609-9a60-3156b1346f57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242911} {:answer/id #uuid "cebd7864-29a0-5f2c-ae2f-532f9334815b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ee90334-9a31-5d11-96f1-a862c794ad22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241596} :answer-field/id #uuid "b037f3a6-dbaa-5eb8-92d4-902e6c52b98d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243754}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "538aef16-d53a-586e-bc6f-7e8857a93b64" :question/math-academy-id "q-109111" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+3xy\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/0d/0dfe132146c4095954d6b628b055317c975fed6ec429b9ad48b68f4015d6c033.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=3xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+xy\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=y.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4392ecc9-1c7a-5314-823d-2a3728f88f79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241789} {:answer/id #uuid "55eae171-7790-51de-a772-4f26b25540d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241953} {:answer/id #uuid "bab79b1a-7547-5a12-bf33-fdf7b3fa5652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242818} {:answer/id #uuid "c716c726-6e2d-518f-81e9-c48a409738ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242941} {:answer/id #uuid "f292d218-8ce3-5932-9bf6-b4fd5ac83454" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4392ecc9-1c7a-5314-823d-2a3728f88f79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241789} :answer-field/id #uuid "78e21ec4-8926-5116-b2a6-997d0e821869" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243672}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c38c694f-327f-51b0-bb98-98b827fdfa0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109112" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),(2,1)$ and $(0,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/8d/8d57fb7f6b8e2e822a11f11a0267994c449c37b43df167a8d48ab8036854b8dd.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{x}^{2})]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)={x}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34fb3361-d343-5c61-a959-37c900d6bd89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2x){e}^{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241643} {:answer/id #uuid "9ab1a6c3-68d4-5733-8fe4-2d2f237787c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242533} {:answer/id #uuid "bfcafdda-b6d1-5664-99bd-4f07a22445bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-y){e}^{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242857} {:answer/id #uuid "e7caa5e6-91d0-52e8-ad17-08e071cfb41a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(xy-{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243231} {:answer/id #uuid "e871b6fc-462a-5b9e-9b8d-327730b6487f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ab1a6c3-68d4-5733-8fe4-2d2f237787c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242533} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1c47a83-1efa-5d30-b13b-23152d473cad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243856}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "809d1894-c8be-5baf-9170-eb9dccb8466c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109114" :question/problem "Consider the quadrilateral $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(1,0),(1,1)$ and $(0,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,dx+2xy{e}^{xy}\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/b7/b7fad52bdf54c0870ad4c4939a314f5657e390b589faf76e60a8d8ef5d926039.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}$ and $Q(x,y)=2xy{e}^{xy}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=(2y+x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=(2y+2x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,dx+2xy{e}^{xy}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[(2y+2x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}-(2y+x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y+2x{y}^{2}-2y-x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23d8c066-7867-5eac-9b9c-1696e4efd675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241492} {:answer/id #uuid "36db06cb-9213-5e4b-8216-0dea5618ec38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241663} {:answer/id #uuid "53428bc9-6dd9-5fe3-9c0f-9fdc6c63f8f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241925} {:answer/id #uuid "c127b9bd-45f4-56f2-b3b5-d08e61034189" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242873} {:answer/id #uuid "f50b50e7-49d5-52e4-8ac0-dfb07852b27a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f50b50e7-49d5-52e4-8ac0-dfb07852b27a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243345} :answer-field/id #uuid "c72e9f53-402d-51aa-8ec3-8bb61e794fdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243791}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "791b6409-d8b7-533d-ab9f-9fd6eeb81e0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109130" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,2),(1,0)$ and $(2,0),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})\\,dx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/8e/8e3efc3f9c58fb0f9a84144e97a300b6ab2a4011d47ee0d2bae1edc8a3f721cd.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})\\,dx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5190752e-a1c8-5dd8-b834-158e58825739" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5190752e-a1c8-5dd8-b834-158e58825739" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362541} :answer-field/id #uuid "02bbd186-1d4e-5461-bc79-ce35c2890910" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362540}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "231df40f-8e86-536b-af50-43d6f14cbda6" :question/math-academy-id "q-169203" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation of $F$ along the border $C$ of the plot $D,$ shown in the diagram above.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Circulation $\\,={∮}_{C}^{}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={∬}_{D}\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{d}A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}{{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Scalar curl of }F}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}{{(3x{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{P}\\,dx+{{(-{y}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{Q}\\,dy\n$$\n\nIn part 2, we found that the scalar curl of $F$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}-6xy\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43fe0455-2f42-5377-bc8e-ce440159c478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415794} {:answer/id #uuid "48f5574c-5c8a-571f-a5ec-a59c2f65571f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415795} {:answer/id #uuid "547db7e0-1aff-5b66-8afe-be02f76901b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415798} {:answer/id #uuid "67711d7b-91ec-5a44-89a3-3e9703cd9cc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415805} {:answer/id #uuid "7c5507aa-5a07-5e59-afae-c1b249eef1f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48f5574c-5c8a-571f-a5ec-a59c2f65571f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415795} :answer-field/id #uuid "8258478b-1136-5626-9c52-3604e6dc2cb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415850}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "25b5bd80-ef51-5481-b2fb-7d3f90359e46" :question/math-academy-id "q-109196" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,1),(0,-1),(1,-1)$ and $(1,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3e81cfacc47dfa241a5fea64de84e375a58a6d86ceab82c86303763d5e41fe5.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-(-2y)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ca40ef0-e014-586e-b378-13e06e23ad16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415800} {:answer/id #uuid "8f26a8d9-946b-54f3-9dd0-a27e7daa977e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415815} {:answer/id #uuid "b1502ceb-1419-5afd-b3f7-e9e6c6df5787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y{cos}^{2}⁡(2xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415822} {:answer/id #uuid "c264dd4e-c055-5e7a-bc5a-8d050645e7d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415827} {:answer/id #uuid "d468b014-e971-5635-97c8-eac3fe909b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x-y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c264dd4e-c055-5e7a-bc5a-8d050645e7d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415827} :answer-field/id #uuid "edcf7bc8-f757-5f28-b389-f3fbae1ad417" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415858}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4af8cfd2-bdac-5f91-b83d-a441b5fa79ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-109199" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(0,-1)$ and $(1,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)\\,dx+2{sin}^{2}⁡(xy)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f4/f4bb6636c104cf17bcd4b25037a3490ba30b7c1380b152c33504d9015903bdca.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)$ and $Q(x,y)=2{sin}^{2}⁡xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)=2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)\\,dx+2{sin}^{2}⁡(xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)-(-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415865 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1373e9bd-bdc4-5ee5-9dd4-483158ca8fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415779} {:answer/id #uuid "4388dab3-b038-5c1c-837a-428bf31f6759" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415793} {:answer/id #uuid "6b66bdb8-a403-5ed8-a631-d9248e4a923d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{e}^{-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415807} {:answer/id #uuid "6d060811-a883-579d-9212-b1daa25ef195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1+{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}){e}^{-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415808} {:answer/id #uuid "c01a8e02-f4d6-5df0-9c30-c6f58e29d206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1373e9bd-bdc4-5ee5-9dd4-483158ca8fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415779} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbfb1dc9-ac7f-533d-9966-b616b6bc9a03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415859}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "581c6229-8cb3-5361-87bc-faf26b91b942" :question/math-academy-id "q-109129" :question/problem "Consider the path $C,$ in the plane, as the boundary of the region enclosed by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the $x$-axis, for $y\\ge 0,$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}x{e}^{-{y}^{2}}\\,dx+{x}^{2}y{e}^{-{y}^{2}}\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/a0/a0d50e8f2faf679e6dee1471c8fa1ca2be4194796e606fc3e89372f0fefe8cfc.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{e}^{-{y}^{2}}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}y{e}^{-{y}^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{e}^{-{y}^{2}}\\,dx+{x}^{2}y{e}^{-{y}^{2}}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}-(-2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}})]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}+2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=4xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24b36ec1-7764-5048-a4d1-c122f6559c8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415783} {:answer/id #uuid "29edb23d-8a02-50c3-b830-5aa8eaaa3f21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415786} {:answer/id #uuid "388b01e6-6a24-58b4-b0fe-57572ce8b425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415788} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8fc7d9-f9fe-58d9-ada0-460b3e833c02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415789} {:answer/id #uuid "f9106e33-884e-5c11-8c6d-9aed7d907a02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "388b01e6-6a24-58b4-b0fe-57572ce8b425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415788} :answer-field/id #uuid "daa742fd-2059-58a9-8c17-15a7e5c8ca78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415857}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "631300c6-4369-57db-a4e4-4ed4d7169680" :question/math-academy-id "q-109120" :question/problem "Consider the path $C,$ in the plane, as the boundary of the region enclosed by the circle ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ and the $y$-axis, for $x\\ge 0,$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}2{y}^{3/2}\\,dx+(x+2x\\sqrt{y})\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/93/93cb529eecb888a110eaad8f55ec458b3710f5a37b497aeed38fa32621178625.png)\n\n \n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2{y}^{3/2}$ and $Q(x,y)=x+2x\\sqrt{y}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3\\sqrt{y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1+2\\sqrt{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}2{y}^{3/2}\\,dx+(x+2x\\sqrt{y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+2\\sqrt{y}-3\\sqrt{y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-\\sqrt{y})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=1-\\sqrt{y}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415871 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1557707f-89c6-567b-bb30-f8c64368ff64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415780} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf53b6c-fab4-56c1-9e1c-7866457b5b5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415790} {:answer/id #uuid "681da06e-fe7e-503f-ae8f-8cbde680fd97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415806} {:answer/id #uuid "73c8af1d-00e0-5e51-be53-dabd553965d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}-x{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415809} {:answer/id #uuid "af98450c-dfab-596e-b54e-440b639e4538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1557707f-89c6-567b-bb30-f8c64368ff64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415780} :answer-field/id #uuid "b75894da-7bf9-54c7-ab7b-ac317b6f0581" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415854}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dfcce989-6529-57a3-8788-c6f5ffffe36a" :question/math-academy-id "q-108830" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(2,0)$ and $(0,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2b/2bec8a0243d2ea9e983cab4c07719ff4a5d309960039ccdd77e621fd15ffd7ed.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}y+y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133883 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133699 :question/id #uuid "2109910f-fc87-5e56-b2f6-12132b9f6073" :question/math-academy-id "e-9072" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C} 4x^2y \\, \\mathrm{d}x+ 2y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y},$ where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(1,2)$ and $(0,2),$ traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/a8/a87f696b1eb42588ab69800b72d821bf47b35676fc19dcd4fe2927bbf36ba3f8.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)= 4x^2y$ and $Q(x,y)= 2y^2.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) : 0 \\leq x \\leq 1,\\, 2x \\leq y \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}4{x}^{2}y\\,dx+2{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(0-4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2x}^{2}(-4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[y]_{2x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(2-2x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(1-x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}\\,dx \\\\  & =-8{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-8(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{12} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11d3f9e1-dad0-51d4-86a9-e0c49465cc3c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a48ca0e-93ed-5174-bab6-4c899fe31648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241997} {:answer/id #uuid "81c43c9e-66cb-55e7-98de-48e2ff68d8cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242317} {:answer/id #uuid "822c3964-a365-5ee7-be32-2b3f5876cb91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242321} {:answer/id #uuid "a81cda8b-cfe4-5e44-8bea-ed48a8910f53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242656} {:answer/id #uuid "c3bb5dcb-f2aa-5a8d-a14d-49c9a860d41c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3bb5dcb-f2aa-5a8d-a14d-49c9a860d41c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242899} :answer-field/id #uuid "14ae7574-9b24-5483-829f-b98eb44bc08b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243471}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4e3c164e-33ad-5643-8072-cdedeec68994" :question/math-academy-id "q-109147" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+({e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(2,2)$ and $(-2,2),$ traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7ca4b6fe3a5a16c4004c75b0e4524cc3b42550cb2413d5b8e1c21a1d53cee902.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-y\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+({e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{-y}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3})dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{3}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{[{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7924d408-b0a6-57f4-9535-29236f3d016b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415810} {:answer/id #uuid "950ad376-e54c-57af-bfa1-45e819b52db4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi ({\\pi }^{2}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415816} {:answer/id #uuid "ab82bc31-acab-5197-b9ab-1bdf4558c405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi ({\\pi }^{2}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415820} {:answer/id #uuid "c5551b46-21d7-59b0-b72c-4198c2a76d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415830} {:answer/id #uuid "d84a9c83-a85a-5496-be1f-846504032a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "950ad376-e54c-57af-bfa1-45e819b52db4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi ({\\pi }^{2}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415816} :answer-field/id #uuid "b60e1b91-b85b-56e9-90f2-353a69264f24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415853}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4381a978-8315-576b-b33a-7ce6b2ca6e4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-41810" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,{x}^{2}-2y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the square $0\\le x\\le \\pi ,0\\le y\\le \\pi ,$ traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/4f/4f75ee554c61dc799273c2c731d608766d27987937d0058df6ee1e0290d8b060.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+({x}^{2}-2y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}-2y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\pi ,0\\le y\\le \\pi \\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+({x}^{2}-2y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{[2xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]}_{0}^{\\pi }\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\pi x+2)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\pi {x}^{2}+2x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{3}+2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi ({\\pi }^{2}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244233 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f857c3f-c4d2-5de0-90f5-abf6baaedfa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241458} {:answer/id #uuid "aab8db49-5b5c-570c-9315-cb19a52f8b78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242672} {:answer/id #uuid "d615277f-623e-5e6d-b251-99bf9abe8109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243076} {:answer/id #uuid "e23d1dd7-1f28-56e1-9512-2ecdb8ec1f35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243188} {:answer/id #uuid "ec87942b-fdaa-51d9-accf-3436cee15850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec87942b-fdaa-51d9-accf-3436cee15850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243271} :answer-field/id #uuid "d34cac99-ce8a-559e-a442-6a5f120b6860" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243807}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5f6efefc-16a2-55cb-8b63-0d3253a6815c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109115" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=(3xy-1)\\,i+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,j$ and $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=2x$ and $y={x}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/5f/5f34ac74599937e0ae181126ff9a3da5b29dfe213614a1f23ca10c7062f22f7d.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(3xy-1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3xy-1$ and $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3xy-1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6x-3x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}3x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x[y]_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x(2x-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}-3{x}^{3})\\,dx \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2(2)^{3}-\\frac{3(2)^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =16-12 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244238 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10dcdef2-642f-55f7-9283-d43907943103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241325} {:answer/id #uuid "54f5783e-24b1-54eb-9c6b-ddf3188e156d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241945} {:answer/id #uuid "de04e406-42a4-5fa6-81c3-f494f888fd31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243155} {:answer/id #uuid "eda54df8-a592-522d-b635-ae2da97bc185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243282} {:answer/id #uuid "edfb2d72-14da-5380-8ba7-43bcec8043f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de04e406-42a4-5fa6-81c3-f494f888fd31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243155} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c1e9f02-a030-5d47-b85b-e2de392e93a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243679}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d2d8f7a5-f5ff-573c-8706-8f2ae5d3ecba" :question/math-academy-id "q-109151" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x}-{y}^{2},{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the square with vertices at $(-1,-1),$ $(1,-1),$ $(1,1)$ and $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/57/574172dda3031b9bb44dbcfb46265453ae543a75bccf8db24e93625e7154bba9.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({e}^{y}+{x}^{2})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{x}-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+{x}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({e}^{y}+{x}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2xy+{y}^{2}]}_{-1}^{1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2x(1)+(1)^{2}-2x(-1)-(-1)^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}4x\\,dx \\\\  & =2{[{x}^{2}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2[(1)^{2}-(-1)^{2}] \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c3ed13a-1aaf-537e-ad51-fca84471105d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241716} {:answer/id #uuid "40e316c5-a282-5acd-80de-6e530f80656c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241761} {:answer/id #uuid "488aaa87-2228-585a-a9ef-8ec1c4e65922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241839} {:answer/id #uuid "81b8abe5-bd3a-5851-8109-3a0b8505d1f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242316} {:answer/id #uuid "90e17c15-a524-5d11-8dd9-16e39337a7ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c3ed13a-1aaf-537e-ad51-fca84471105d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241716} :answer-field/id #uuid "2637cc62-8f36-5554-86dd-89b757f3c3f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243507}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "13e80aba-bf74-59e1-8677-06e92124ae97" :question/math-academy-id "q-109160" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}(2y-x)\\,dx+(5x-y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=x$ and $y={x}^{2}-x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/e7/e788bbcbda4cc715c6989b7de9d08d60e3b0d178eeb9432a342a4c86c4c01b86.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2y-x$ and $Q(x,y)=5x-y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}-x\\le y\\le x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2y-x)\\,dx+(5x-y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5-2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}-x}^{x}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}[y]_{{x}^{2}-x}^{x}\\,dx \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & =3{[{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3(4-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06018ec3-eeb1-582c-9045-74896f54fbce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{81}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241232} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb0cba9-41d0-58fa-ac43-9d0d6348605b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242570} {:answer/id #uuid "aec24a0e-77a6-5f7d-bf26-bf988d8624c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242703} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f58ec7-1d48-577a-a783-f801a669183c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243099} {:answer/id #uuid "eda11e16-3812-5727-9763-9ffb41a27ea6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06018ec3-eeb1-582c-9045-74896f54fbce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{81}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241232} :answer-field/id #uuid "215f201b-1149-55e4-a109-5b440565fb18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243501}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d29c3a3a-1bb9-50bf-8673-d429ca766812" :question/math-academy-id "q-109164" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}2xy\\,dx+{y}^{2}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}$ and $y=\\sqrt{3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/eb/eb0b120cc4d63c8dab18c8acb37f5835e99db746584262f6bb33e8ca99b6a16d.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2xy$ and $Q(x,y)={y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{3x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}2xy\\,dx+{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(0-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}/3}^{\\sqrt{3x}}(-2x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2x)[y]_{{x}^{2}/3}^{\\sqrt{3x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2x)(\\sqrt{3x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})dx \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\sqrt{3}{x}^{3/2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})dx \\\\  & =-2{[\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{3x}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{12}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{2(3)^{2}\\sqrt{3(3)}}{5}-\\frac{(3)^{4}}{12}) \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{54}{5}-\\frac{27}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{81}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3085d7de-40fa-57f6-a2ed-934cac75c3ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{27}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241613} {:answer/id #uuid "53c19e60-39f0-59ab-83fb-c81560577c2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{64}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241930} {:answer/id #uuid "575f8117-55cc-5d5d-a960-2240c1431e26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241973} {:answer/id #uuid "62e9d241-31b4-57e7-8bb2-435dfd18433b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242068} {:answer/id #uuid "f3465b0d-4022-519a-a8c5-5e9237e0a9cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3465b0d-4022-519a-a8c5-5e9237e0a9cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243336} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aac6df8-0c7c-51ef-9bef-7baf99240a0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243619}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7203c090-0409-569d-a6e2-3e62f7801a90" :question/math-academy-id "q-109166" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨2x+{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+2y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=3.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/fa/fafd281fc63fb276c9759fd84f722096d494aa3e3fe9441f43aa6ecd1f9dfda9.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x+{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}/3}(2x-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[2xy-{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{{x}^{2}/3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{9})dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{45}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)^{4}}{6}-\\frac{(3)^{5}}{45} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{27}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326911 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c5474dd-5be4-50d8-bebe-7eb5eb66bf7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324460} {:answer/id #uuid "44ecd8ea-bdce-5d1f-a1a0-626d659605b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324527} {:answer/id #uuid "d8cd2f91-5b3e-58ed-b480-0e9af6226381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325857} {:answer/id #uuid "de55e393-f59f-5979-bf5b-51218edda1ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325912} {:answer/id #uuid "f5723cad-795b-55bb-8ab0-563ed1f0d4a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c5474dd-5be4-50d8-bebe-7eb5eb66bf7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324460} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c9e60ff-5c2c-5bd5-a03b-c43d94b21994" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326376}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "63bc1464-04bd-5198-8418-9e8745d1335c" :question/math-academy-id "q-41811" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{3}+2{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(2,0)$ and $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/73/73bf9b973e50c540a873f4f1a44d936474e4c49d7041a330902508a840645009.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{3}+2{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):y\\le x\\le 2-y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}-4xy]}_{y}^{2-y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-y)^{2}-4(2-y)y-{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{2}-12y+4)\\,dy \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2}-3y+1)\\,dy \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}+y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{3}{2}+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415863 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2973f614-b513-5cf6-8c17-61bf7293f111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415785} {:answer/id #uuid "3e7a1a9b-014d-571b-8c35-beddd81764b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415791} {:answer/id #uuid "614e19a5-0156-5aa6-92f4-46816789f3b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415801} {:answer/id #uuid "c3cc7632-9b26-5a7b-bf16-9202ce5e54a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415828} {:answer/id #uuid "f51732c9-6bbf-50cf-aa38-918f942e3f4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "614e19a5-0156-5aa6-92f4-46816789f3b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415801} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6016823-928e-5a70-98df-762771d654fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415852}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4383616d-c261-5116-9385-9c929fedb95e" :question/math-academy-id "q-109157" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}(2x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dx+(x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the trapezoid with vertices at $(0,1),$ $(0,-5),$ $(4,-1)$ and $(4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/ac/acd7ae0cc90007b9f710f242ab1f0498b2439cdbb8951df388b9d8d0588d553f.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(2x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dx+(x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =1+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 4,\\,x-5\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dx+(x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{x-5}^{1}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[y]_{x-5}^{1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(6-x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[6x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =24-\\frac{16}{2} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bd1f5ef-c9ed-5994-83b0-1b7bfde99771" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415799} {:answer/id #uuid "64f04a8e-60e7-5cb7-93ee-ee43e5f4bcee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415803} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0a07cf-1f8b-5b69-8fec-824801c47fec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415814} {:answer/id #uuid "f37da209-7b24-5d10-b640-c3d7580c8d8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415840} {:answer/id #uuid "fd217b62-f697-5f47-89d1-aa9d6c12c868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64f04a8e-60e7-5cb7-93ee-ee43e5f4bcee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415803} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3408749-b613-5708-9ee5-7482588129d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415856}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5bd170e0-be10-5a08-9034-fb1371f7f685" :question/math-academy-id "q-41475" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨6{x}^{2}-xy+11,{y}^{2}+1⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(2,-1)$ and $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/00/007634ca34871f05cde9d44a58fcf24b1e5fd3b8c337c68f2025fb8724eef819.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(6{x}^{2}-xy+11)\\,dx+({y}^{2}+1)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6{x}^{2}-xy+11$ and $Q(x,y)={y}^{2}+1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,-\\frac{x}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{x}{2}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(6{x}^{2}-xy+11)\\,dx+({y}^{2}+1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[0-(-x)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-x/2}^{x/2}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x[y]_{-x/2}^{x/2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x(x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "008b0a85-ca8c-5ca9-bac7-3b09c846bdfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415776} {:answer/id #uuid "1752e0da-3afa-549b-a738-e3248a7caa51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415781} {:answer/id #uuid "8a4d1306-f937-5ce3-9c78-97d29184dca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415813} {:answer/id #uuid "a73d50e9-baec-56fe-b31f-f9d4aaf5eb2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415819} {:answer/id #uuid "cac62874-d5b4-593b-8361-008219384519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{31}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a4d1306-f937-5ce3-9c78-97d29184dca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415813} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9adf5ad-146a-5b8c-a73a-531cc3071103" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415855}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6fbdc55e-ae46-5858-9840-dcfa0a36807f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109149" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}xy\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}+y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the rectangle with vertices at $(-1,0),$ $(3,0),$ $(3,2)$ and $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/39/3954846460076fa1ef1e5826d32bde9145e7350cb2324072b001d8c6ff41cc14.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=xy$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}xy\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}+y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4x-x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}3x[y]_{0}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}3x(2)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}6x\\,dx \\\\  & =3[{x}^{2}]_{-1}^{3} \\\\  & =3[9-1] \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415869 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bb0f3ad-fee3-562b-9e95-4b5539d42793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415787} {:answer/id #uuid "61c02992-1335-5c6e-a197-06ab323b3dc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415802} {:answer/id #uuid "b224effd-33ce-5fb0-9db8-2bd01771711f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415823} {:answer/id #uuid "b4dfbed0-cc00-5564-812f-23a1d3638249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415824} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e38dea-a3e8-5c30-a757-acb922de2e15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3e38dea-a3e8-5c30-a757-acb922de2e15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415829} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dc55032-e087-5612-84b6-a80c771dff9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415849}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8c2f5a35-b3df-565f-bebb-8a3475dfd0d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-109144" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}(2xy-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2x)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(-1,0),$ $(1,0)$ and $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/cb/cb85e51584f0c41fc19210a5ce56289baa652aff3046d5d88c034d187b7e45dd.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2xy-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+2x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2xy-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2-2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(2+2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2y+{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1-x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2(1-x)+(1-x)^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}-4x+3)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-2{x}^{2}+3x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}-2+3]-[-\\frac{1}{3}-2-3] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals Using Green's Theorem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179645 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179483 :question/id #uuid "15426c37-1508-5407-ab49-b26f8761795c" :question/math-academy-id "e-15148" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = 8y\\,\\mathbf i + 4x\\,\\mathbf j$ and the circle $C$ given by $x^2+y^2 = 4.$ If the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by  \n\n![](images/9d/9d24a5f692535453b1b34c2bc9236bd44cd2c88c05724a2a5dbdf804b98957e5.svg)\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $\\mathbf n = \\dfrac x2\\,\\mathbf i + \\dfrac y2\\,\\mathbf j.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff84680c55f5349431cf3d70066eeaba2c680569faa3b2210e5d14ef63ccd99e.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(8y\\,i+4x\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j)\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}4xy+2xy\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}6xy\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $6xy.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9c8ca56-5ea8-547c-80ab-6b1f1347447e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180034 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68b2771c-3bf6-5560-b718-7272a58b3b05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260790} {:answer/id #uuid "736d4540-14f3-51e5-83fc-72912b452936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260884} {:answer/id #uuid "a638c5e9-524c-5a5b-8853-188f7d08eba7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261321} {:answer/id #uuid "bb564d9a-1d00-56fb-a8bb-81270d654610" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261524} {:answer/id #uuid "c00b58a6-3000-5270-ae8d-919718716fca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}-{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c00b58a6-3000-5270-ae8d-919718716fca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}-{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261572} :answer-field/id #uuid "9380c67a-4a39-5f09-adf3-46f93da5f70a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262387}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ba1404b-6716-5e6e-975f-e1670e68a51c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126833" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=x\\,i+y\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/38/386f010932b5846e7f47a605a94a5b5d580bd35dbe37512b1583d15d29f3dc70.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (xi+y\\,j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}{x}^{3}-{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{3}-{y}^{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180035 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0256a8e6-ff43-5ac8-af7e-6b20461a1f86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259891} {:answer/id #uuid "31a4a27e-adae-5b71-a64f-340e4d163c52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260321} {:answer/id #uuid "458c9e50-9ca7-5a83-a136-566c24bdb806" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260481} {:answer/id #uuid "7ef68d7c-1da2-5bcb-9618-02f37f9bc30a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260975} {:answer/id #uuid "c93f48b0-cdb7-5f6f-b78d-d76054d5d2fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "458c9e50-9ca7-5a83-a136-566c24bdb806" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260481} :answer-field/id #uuid "8418b50e-8c27-58c7-87d6-b5b2519f456a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262350}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee07bcc4-1a59-560b-bb9b-97cdb795faec" :question/math-academy-id "q-126850" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b37c10064003374c146c58bba99ff55304529ee66c9fb38c44246ccc90e8f402.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{1}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=i.$\n\n![](images/c4/c4c4f2f3d5b6188ac5a6ec41bb96fc0c7132cc39308cebfa16ee759eabac2c75.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}({x}^{2}\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235531 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7a3a887-6de4-535d-9d08-7728c412ff9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7a3a887-6de4-535d-9d08-7728c412ff9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234859} :answer-field/id #uuid "48e9ca1b-260a-561e-bcfd-839e7bc6578a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235046}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8217520-113c-5745-9a02-5051b4dbb017" :question/math-academy-id "q-168603" :question/problem "![](images/64/642a546fc498dfb0f01c0729959f9f68b8dc2d142d966673f204eda910f19211.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Consider the square $C$ with side length $8$ centered at $O,$ shown above. The flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}^{}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}s.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{1}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=i.$\n\n![](images/19/1939a274191b89f69e51a929654489ed9038df0313f6e47b15e0ff55ad813066.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(({y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}{y}^{3}-1\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aced5e1-8641-5fed-a199-e5810c31368a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260252} {:answer/id #uuid "36d6919c-ff3a-5148-ab21-32b03a24ba12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260366} {:answer/id #uuid "902a1d36-1807-54ae-be76-2c49e245e66d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261128} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d705d7-3e90-5c01-b0ee-80cf3f83501a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261303} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e25d3a-5b47-51f9-a030-43d19298c404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{4}{y}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3d705d7-3e90-5c01-b0ee-80cf3f83501a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261303} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8e22ec6-b427-52ff-a35f-b4cc82a25268" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262459}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a35f06e-a951-5ad4-b83c-3bd955e95960" :question/math-academy-id "q-127049" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=2{x}^{2}\\,i+2{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=\\frac{x}{2}\\,i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff84680c55f5349431cf3d70066eeaba2c680569faa3b2210e5d14ef63ccd99e.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(2{x}^{2}\\,i+2{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j)\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{3}+{y}^{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064ac3bd-68bf-5d7e-96ef-f16e2f295add" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259931} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb4f557-d11e-580e-bee0-d908cc4fd0ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260543} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4ba798-036b-5fb5-b057-c5ec468f5bee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260676} {:answer/id #uuid "747cb36c-e574-588b-b6da-8d1419ba5e23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260894} {:answer/id #uuid "efd7be33-e0d4-5074-ac91-c596c897aa80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efd7be33-e0d4-5074-ac91-c596c897aa80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261980} :answer-field/id #uuid "055a7271-4a54-504f-8c0f-5f25db56cbdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262132}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd38dff4-6be5-5901-81f2-c7b236232887" :question/math-academy-id "q-127050" :question/problem "![](images/4f/4f8af75aece2346ffbf7a09e1ee90b6e0270f5661dd74e068abb2b2a848e5b97.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{3}\\,i+3{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{2}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $y$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=j.$ \n\n![](images/a8/a83c3476bbe2ed2872ed8938dda59c5d0e6d58d05ecaf662748e7a184a4994a4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}({x}^{3}\\,i+3{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $3{y}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ff58a9-31d9-52b6-9539-5a96afef5cb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259913} {:answer/id #uuid "87cf9463-02d9-5650-9a7a-bd75cf50def2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261058} {:answer/id #uuid "98e4b0c5-5690-5070-9b90-1bf0473522bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261200} {:answer/id #uuid "a27a5529-cca2-57ba-871d-ed34369afc60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261291} {:answer/id #uuid "c4317848-9295-5ac7-92a2-f763f5f1951c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a27a5529-cca2-57ba-871d-ed34369afc60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261291} :answer-field/id #uuid "27741482-00f8-5769-86bf-3cde1eeab0b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262195}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e37a59b-ba01-56a0-982c-60d7797b3b3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-127053" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d9663a0a40f3c3860cce0712e9e9aab41d55251ea15fca33ec663515af57d10.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{3}y\\,i-x{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the rectangle $C,$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{3}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=-i.$\n\n![](images/c6/c6dd493e02633854184be1be0f38246e1e9dce2949f9cc1aa93b943c91d1e44c.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}({x}^{3}y\\,i-x{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (-i)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}-{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $-{x}^{3}y.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "299aaae1-8475-55bf-8b13-35a76234f367" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy+{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260241} {:answer/id #uuid "4221d1d5-a811-5dd0-9dcb-746ffd0e2961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260453} {:answer/id #uuid "7493e335-b4cb-504c-9d89-5d43ba48df15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x{y}^{2}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260895} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6b2b2c-adf6-5af1-ac56-fcf71743fa7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy+3x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261100} {:answer/id #uuid "fa50487d-228a-5944-9440-c96410e564fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4221d1d5-a811-5dd0-9dcb-746ffd0e2961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260453} :answer-field/id #uuid "dafc80d4-38a2-5de1-ac74-dfe54c6268a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262503}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a3880ec-3713-5f30-bb66-5de9c6716caf" :question/math-academy-id "q-127056" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=15xy\\,i+5{x}^{2}\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=\\frac{x}{5}\\,i+\\frac{y}{5}\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/d3/d3d68329d81150c4a975bae4963d4e480cefaef0f2a0a29a0648c3793e9fb462.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(15xy\\,i+5{x}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{x}{5}\\,i+\\frac{y}{5}\\,j)\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}3{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}4{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $4{x}^{2}y.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "137ef58b-7aa2-56ab-acf1-569d978ae99d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260048} {:answer/id #uuid "47760d48-4b08-58db-9ae0-5ef94c117ea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260496} {:answer/id #uuid "759ce143-e5ea-5feb-a66f-7d6fef516f96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260905} {:answer/id #uuid "adc6fab4-f58a-5a62-ae5e-15a4b5933803" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261403} {:answer/id #uuid "c1221186-e4d1-5209-8c59-aae7eb1e9afa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47760d48-4b08-58db-9ae0-5ef94c117ea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260496} :answer-field/id #uuid "9252b2c4-87e1-538b-b027-ba338b40221c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262382}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0d38139-02d0-5e66-aef1-2d1bcd0dc8e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-127057" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,i+2{x}^{3}y\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by  \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=\\frac{x}{2}\\,i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff84680c55f5349431cf3d70066eeaba2c680569faa3b2210e5d14ef63ccd99e.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,i+2{x}^{3}y\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j)\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0368d3ef-7ff9-5da6-857d-96776c228a1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0368d3ef-7ff9-5da6-857d-96776c228a1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362498} :answer-field/id #uuid "47f7b160-4a23-58c9-94a1-d65bba0f866a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362497}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d35a688-8b95-5b4b-a5d5-a0c9f931f061" :question/math-academy-id "q-167889" :question/problem "Water flows over the element $E$ according to the vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=6y\\,i+x\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $E$ found in part 5 has zero $k$-component, it may be considered a two-dimensional vector defined in terms of $i$ and $j$ only. Use this to find an expression for the flux of $F$ across the element in terms of $x$ and $y.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Flux = \n${∮}_{E}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}s.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $E$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $E$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{E}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit vector to $E.$\n\n![](images/2c/2cd5e4c7ab86cdb999e239e986713a51c07b8decb629b1ee4a656077590f04bb.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{E}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{E}(6y\\,i+x\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{x}{3}\\,i+\\frac{y}{3}\\,j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{E}2xy+\\frac{1}{3}xy\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{E}\\frac{7}{3}xy\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420909 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64bb62e3-bd57-57a6-9c6c-17776849a043" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420872} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5de1a3-32b4-5e14-81b4-8c30c59f2613" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{5}{y}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420873} {:answer/id #uuid "794f9e41-d152-5887-a25c-abf259a83c57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420877} {:answer/id #uuid "cf225ba4-1606-5bb5-954a-0c37ef35f638" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420890} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5e5cf8-0499-54ef-8365-3f3efa38105a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e5de1a3-32b4-5e14-81b4-8c30c59f2613" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{5}{y}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420873} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4055bff-eb04-5d8c-8a7f-fd7faf3e0c1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420906}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07e888b6-1046-5ef1-87ef-0610d3939941" :question/math-academy-id "q-127058" :question/problem "![](images/65/653699a9c78577272425de7f8e6d015743e2c2e8c6e9a21bef189dc06fd80684.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{5}{y}^{7}\\,i+x{y}^{3}\\,j$ and the rectangle $C$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{1}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=i.$\n\n![](images/35/35e33731fbe85305a4308ef03c9f793b77558d66350d9345c30e643df1c40ba1.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}({x}^{5}{y}^{7}\\,i+x{y}^{3}\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}{x}^{5}{y}^{7}\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{5}{y}^{7}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02b163d2-8d88-51ac-abaa-42c491e74881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420855} {:answer/id #uuid "0caccbf9-4642-59fa-8f70-8c1bac70ef51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420858} {:answer/id #uuid "1148ef69-d315-5d49-9d48-0b6ccac5465c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-y)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420860} {:answer/id #uuid "d961af8e-4079-56ce-98d9-0bbc2b4be0c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420894} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0b8003-7828-51fb-91cc-f49acd49d338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(x-y)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1148ef69-d315-5d49-9d48-0b6ccac5465c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-y)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420860} :answer-field/id #uuid "544df328-c7fc-55b8-b971-da80f2afa474" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420901}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71fbebcb-93ff-5be9-84c7-d1676b609367" :question/math-academy-id "q-127059" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b08e4cc9dd6ebde7f4bf544d7a76c4706dbd69a2c31d4c0fda8f816d4f871b5.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)=(x+y)^{2}\\,i+(x-y)^{2}\\,j$ and the rectangle $C$  shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{2}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $y$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=j.$ \n\n![](images/5d/5d0e9699f75e1f3f727c5c771fdce1a66311496cb55d31c0aac789901feed0cb.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}((x+y)^{2}\\,i+(x-y)^{2}\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(x-y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $(x-y)^{2}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression for Flux"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179647 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179485 :question/id #uuid "3f592794-e0e9-529c-9040-7b2806de2380" :question/math-academy-id "e-15150" :question/problem "![](images/59/59b5f1676f036f80e6aa2e9e331327ed9ec28e88bacda52a91ee0865736e9ce3.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F = 5x\\,\\mathbf i - 3y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $4$ and height $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$ \n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/79/793755cda5243dbb427d8722c21ab150a4821c3957f0ccd0ef0fedbbd576e924.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(5x\\,i-3y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = 2\\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =5{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_3$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s = 20.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side $C_2.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_2 = \\mathbf j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(5x\\,i-3y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = t\\,\\mathbf i + \\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf i, \\qquad t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s = -12.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s= 2 \\cdot 20 + 2 \\cdot (-12) = 16.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "446c5e28-00d3-5a17-ad90-0e66b5ec3a59" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180037 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c270d16-d1b9-584c-a753-2613c93b2733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177892} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7ecac2-757d-5844-815f-910c23411410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177893} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2cb1ed-8729-57d0-bfa3-5b2591155680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177894} {:answer/id #uuid "0185a290-822d-58a0-ae4e-dfba4ef2a94a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177895} {:answer/id #uuid "0a7388ca-c74f-5074-90cf-b3f8cb2908d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177896}] :answer-field/id #uuid "15b24aa2-07c6-5f2c-85f4-99a1708ccb2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177891}] :question/id #uuid "2e3c5efb-b46d-57f5-8660-cecbe88198e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-126892" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4e47604540f3cfb3f73af34b0d3eb64bb649e9a42004d22eccc2dd9007de4fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F = 2xi + yj$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C$.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0b425eb-22e7-5126-9c59-1b5123683fa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177928} {:answer/id #uuid "12c2cb4f-61be-53d6-a2c3-6683264b610e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177929} {:answer/id #uuid "302cc11d-b1c7-5509-a279-663acfb5cc82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177930} {:answer/id #uuid "307bb29b-0e2c-55ac-acbb-7cf8129ef5d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177931} {:answer/id #uuid "3254ddc0-ccb7-5590-9971-d8a69bbb023a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177932}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f52107b4-b94f-5cca-902b-99cbc154821a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177927}] :question/id #uuid "99c9a666-1234-5907-897c-c576caf3c171" :question/math-academy-id "q-127205" :question/problem "![](images/96/9610c7c482b561f4893b562bb6cf891136b30bea20c73fd56caa9a1a12a7a088.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F =-3xi - 2yj$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $4$ and height $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C$.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262727 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61102a14-b533-5513-a723-c254c2d06f5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260709} {:answer/id #uuid "786a7883-0cb6-590c-8f39-483e7b2f4ad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260927} {:answer/id #uuid "917128b1-1f2a-5c09-b649-63ccf0fa63f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261139} {:answer/id #uuid "9e4f88ee-8685-5a3d-b096-fe5bfea05166" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261247} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd22cf0-a19c-5a79-a9fa-e7478861a8af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e4f88ee-8685-5a3d-b096-fe5bfea05166" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261247} :answer-field/id #uuid "34367921-de6d-539e-8aa1-920b58ce0e4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262217}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2de65327-4951-549c-b9fb-03c2c7cc11a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-127200" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b652e9eae16ae78a06743e75513674cc6d7596aadf2bd07aca4398a99089348.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=3x\\,i-y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(3x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=6.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(3x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 6+2\\cdot (-2)=8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262729 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67f5177e-6bf6-5a90-909c-5c40f83cd489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260782} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5b7209-20d7-55b0-99b9-dc9f4fc2af0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261099} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc02a0a-836e-58f2-9074-006f830abe44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261527} {:answer/id #uuid "dd252ef2-e9bb-5a70-8099-f8ffbfed03d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261830} {:answer/id #uuid "fea486a1-e02a-50be-9661-d1979f5e7aba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fea486a1-e02a-50be-9661-d1979f5e7aba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262107} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f2eae53-5bcf-5b7f-af5d-eafcc9d581c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262409}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b49bddc7-bed1-5bef-8ddb-d1f67820d61a" :question/math-academy-id "q-127207" :question/problem "![](images/66/6696cf798bb4ecdf8c6eaeb65b0bb49477b04207c22bceff1e2cdee8614b1728.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=x\\,i-2y\\,j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $2$ and height $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/aba4a2e6603e65d3f67e65c5af73b2cdda6bdf798d9e9d09005f738d700500ea.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+2\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-8.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 4+2\\cdot (-8)=-8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "152fede1-5726-517f-a05e-876200b5ee78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "156" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288176} {:answer/id #uuid "413223d4-8a5c-5d2b-9abe-668e2bd30c32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288475} {:answer/id #uuid "77244b17-d797-5c25-af3e-b7be4b5247e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288853} {:answer/id #uuid "f4aa3f09-ac58-5db0-a8cf-92e3e9290ba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "164" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289645} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc32f2b-2e06-5d37-ad35-f23957cef930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77244b17-d797-5c25-af3e-b7be4b5247e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288853} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f51012a-ecf2-53cc-b685-8df57cc4cd3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289768}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5afdf08-64c4-5039-ae6a-14029973d009" :question/math-academy-id "q-226133" :question/problem "![](images/2d/2dcfb8778adf7e9a05ff1dff886d0d8290427ed2ae910b46db4fe9aeb36170eb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=x\\,i+3y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/f2/f283fe675e0a9ef736947e404f03ab5afb90a4209ba1a6949d18ac6d3fb20048.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(x\\,i+3y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=18.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(x\\,i+3y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+3\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=54.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 18+2\\cdot 54=144.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290431 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68c3c913-6ba1-589f-b840-0c1c159c5673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288765} {:answer/id #uuid "87c909c6-4a8b-53ba-9fe3-85ea04806d51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288947} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca45b03-5e4b-5dc7-a0d5-d0be258cb0e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288972} {:answer/id #uuid "98530f43-0be2-5253-982a-5e1438c34a83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289053} {:answer/id #uuid "d44d97a4-ddc4-54ff-bcc7-703ddeca5526" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87c909c6-4a8b-53ba-9fe3-85ea04806d51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288947} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cd839a4-a88e-5b41-ab67-448f00d8b008" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290041}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3cf6fcba-4714-5b05-9571-4d5f1a493fd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-226135" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec48931b53248128f4eefd2fe2428120adeef983b6fd2a613e7dcca96c00fe12.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=2x\\,i-y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/86/867ab8337894fa75d010bcb12557702059a4a93aaa2bdef2f853c46ff2801d67.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(2x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-3}^{3}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=36.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(2x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+3\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-3}^{3}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-18.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 36+2\\cdot (-18)=36.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a7f4b5-7cd8-5940-8c69-846081503ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288372} {:answer/id #uuid "88197c07-3a6b-5987-bb1c-74af6ddc7d2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288949} {:answer/id #uuid "8fdaa769-2e35-5ecc-99d5-dfff81635172" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288995} {:answer/id #uuid "b789b18f-9e62-5ef5-849d-af6b3ac4df59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289248} {:answer/id #uuid "dc728a29-5158-5872-a5a0-0b92e91b195a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc728a29-5158-5872-a5a0-0b92e91b195a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289499} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8ca8232-836f-5ef4-a146-93cc8fbacbb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290106}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d0758f2-17c6-502d-ba56-8303db371c67" :question/math-academy-id "q-226149" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a62dfaec4e967433ef56d319e4e66a220441a711ce97a84ca96a4bbd7d0bf37.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=2x\\,i-3y\\,j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $4$ and height $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/00/00a9df76d0be8ffd33b2ee968b57a21eb1e6947af6e94505313f163b584f5769.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(2x\\,i-3y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=8.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(2x\\,i-3y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-12.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 8+2\\cdot (-12)=-8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dabc53b-7f14-580e-9dfc-62c43ae6e5b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288220} {:answer/id #uuid "522030d5-4b17-54f8-8958-12fdb0032b09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288601} {:answer/id #uuid "7ffee840-90f4-5267-a69d-56a7b110e4ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288897} {:answer/id #uuid "9794e61c-293d-58a5-913c-74da807b4f9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289049} {:answer/id #uuid "ad33f942-65c1-5719-bd3e-bdd804dc2bf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad33f942-65c1-5719-bd3e-bdd804dc2bf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289179} :answer-field/id #uuid "163d16b5-420f-5024-8996-baf2ca361651" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289782}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6848eb02-e01e-5364-9fcf-7d0ce934cecc" :question/math-academy-id "q-226150" :question/problem "![](images/80/80cc5e165f78bc1fc8cc7f83644fd00ca4a8f4e05e69d97b654bb2f1450d9b99.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-x\\,i+3y\\,j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $6$ and height $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/511bae61d710c3362bb18618717b3d44ef5dcedece5f669e0b5237b7b524a054.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-x\\,i+3y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=3i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=-12.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(-x\\,i+3y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+2\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=36.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot (-12)+2\\cdot 36=48.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290435 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05bced42-9d9b-51eb-a8d9-8037582027ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288075} {:answer/id #uuid "2d72cffe-4c07-5c70-b05b-180492dc12a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288334} {:answer/id #uuid "47db62fc-8131-5663-b17b-7d3455d7dd0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288527} {:answer/id #uuid "648ce239-aa26-55b4-97d5-fe6bb326ae67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288743} {:answer/id #uuid "8a83f196-f6c0-5e78-9d8c-b4264e812e15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47db62fc-8131-5663-b17b-7d3455d7dd0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288527} :answer-field/id #uuid "a03be787-385b-5f52-9f16-af8df82818b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290084}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a69a7a8b-52c6-5396-ab13-a2f6e97bdfa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-226152" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0ac0fa01b3e09d268115a604a53ca6247c9e26d88603482223aa43927df20561.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-2x\\,i+5y\\,j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $2$ and height $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/29/29f4b57751e27f01539fb48acf115b6e255d47a04ee647ef85aa496731b266c8.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-2x\\,i+5y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=-8.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(-2x\\,i+5y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+2\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =5{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=20.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot (-8)+2\\cdot 20=24.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa1db5b-3f41-577b-8ee0-5f7105de71c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420472} {:answer/id #uuid "0c5de29c-bfc1-53ce-83a7-41993dcf260c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420473} {:answer/id #uuid "615383ae-5073-5a27-a6bc-b222285b07e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420480} {:answer/id #uuid "7860d733-39e7-5d34-930a-375944911a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420483} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e0b03b-9a26-5f35-8452-4cc76772c98a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7860d733-39e7-5d34-930a-375944911a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420483} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc977685-7a6f-5892-9d86-65a9ccbd87da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420493}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6071adb1-29d3-5cda-a599-699b41d64b95" :question/math-academy-id "q-226151" :question/problem "![](images/de/de3e018fa1382ee2250d1b2e256e3b47cafef346852007f0e6afb84342d7cb04.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=3x\\,i-2y\\,j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $4$ and height $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/09/0950c559d5d6df93cf40afd8fdbd07f090f864fa462083bfd496246e7f2bded9.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(3x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=36.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(3x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+3j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-24.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 36+2\\cdot (-24)=24.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420916 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10472c8b-0f2c-541f-a4f7-87022439fc30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420859} {:answer/id #uuid "57aeb9a4-34ac-5fdf-99f7-f349f66e9ca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420869} {:answer/id #uuid "721de4ee-1215-587c-a230-6395ae1c468d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420874} {:answer/id #uuid "83ba3fd3-59b5-593e-9d3a-53cc62cc9e50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420879} {:answer/id #uuid "8fa05c49-f0b6-5d89-a641-050254ac8771" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57aeb9a4-34ac-5fdf-99f7-f349f66e9ca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420869} :answer-field/id #uuid "a01086e0-9b32-569e-ab08-30e8248e546d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420905}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab737fa4-bfd8-53e2-9695-4ad4cbdcb6c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-226136" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9da6bdb9ab5f93d0b0d11dfd60ab2b647380a94ce337c2314ec3504cabafa889.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=4x\\,i-3y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(4x\\,i-3y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=8.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(4x\\,i-3y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-6.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 8+2\\cdot (-6)=4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03305bbd-2ed9-5a18-8c72-9c0d0c631c78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421464} {:answer/id #uuid "78c29a36-9fe5-5900-be19-eb34c038c994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421465} {:answer/id #uuid "97e1e9e0-3fe5-5a2d-b26c-2e7b570ac0c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421466} {:answer/id #uuid "e135fd94-1acc-52d5-ad78-5d64f964e29a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421467} {:answer/id #uuid "e888d5f3-7dc6-5267-ad8c-2c21bc41dcbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e135fd94-1acc-52d5-ad78-5d64f964e29a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421467} :answer-field/id #uuid "600bf7ec-a1e3-56fd-89d4-4751fb47440c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421469}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9f7d0a1-c461-5247-ab30-95b875686e93" :question/math-academy-id "q-226134" :question/problem "![](images/95/958965484cd271c5eb7ccfc8bf913386e57b9b733719885d232e562063969ca8.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-x\\,i+2y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-x\\,i+2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=-8.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(-x\\,i+2y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+2\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=16.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot (-8)+2\\cdot 16=16.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Rectangle Using Two Symmetry Properties"}]}
